# Rust
target/

# Local LLM weights (~1 GB — download per README)
*.gguf

# Local OCR model weights (ocrs/rten — ~12 MB, auto-downloaded per README)
*.rten

# synthpass-bench's generated JSON reports — generated, not hand-edited/committed
bench-report*.json
provider-bench-report*.json

# Web app upload/working directory (may contain uploaded PII)
work/

# samples/ specimen IMAGES are not tracked on main -- they live on the
# orphan `samples-data` branch (see scripts/sync-samples.ps1), the same
# isolated-worktree pattern bench-data uses, so a bulk corpus refresh never
# grows main's history. Only the small hand-verified ground truth stays
# tracked here: samples/README.md and samples/ocr_fixtures/*.json + *.md.
# Run `./scripts/sync-samples.ps1` after a fresh clone to populate images.
samples/passports/
samples/id_cards/
samples/driving_licenses/
samples/misc/
samples/ocr_fixtures/*
!samples/ocr_fixtures/*.json
!samples/ocr_fixtures/*.md

# "No PII in logs or fixtures" (CONTRIBUTING.md) still applies to whatever
# lands in samples-data: refuse anything marked Private by name, as a second
# line of defence behind "keep it in work/ instead". samples/ no longer being
# tracked on main doesn't relax this -- scripts/sync-samples.ps1 and
# scripts/ingest-fetched-samples.ps1 both operate against local samples/, and
# a stray manual `git add -A` inside the samples-data worktree is exactly the
# accident this guards against.
samples/**/*Private*

# The real-specimen corpus (except samples/ocr_fixtures/, which is committed —
# see samples/README.md) is not tracked: git keeps every byte forever, and a
# multi-machine local mirror doesn't need git for that. Fetch/regenerate via
# tools/fetch_commons_mrz_specimens_v2.py, or copy from another machine.
samples/passports/
samples/id_cards/
samples/driving_licenses/
samples/misc/

# v0.3.0 work-in-progress drafts (not yet wired into the build)
drafts/

# wasm-pack build output (built in CI for GitHub Pages)
crates/mrz-wasm/pkg/

# cargo-fuzz crash/timeout artifacts and its own Cargo.lock (the seed corpus
# under fuzz/corpus/ IS committed — only discovered-crash reproducers aren't).
fuzz/artifacts/
fuzz/Cargo.lock

web/pkg/

# Demo OCR runtime — fetched + SHA-256-verified at deploy time by
# web/fetch-vendor.sh, never committed (mirrors the .rten model pattern)
web/vendor/
web/tessdata/eng.traineddata.gz

# Generated synthetic documents (synthpass generate --out-dir out/)
/out/

# Bulk Wikimedia Commons fetch staging (tools/fetch_*.py). Deliberately NOT
# committed, same reasoning as samples/ itself now living on samples-data
# rather than main: the fetchers pull whole Commons categories, and git keeps
# every byte forever, so a bulk dump is a permanent cost for a staging area
# whose whole purpose is to be sifted.
#
# The fetch scripts themselves ARE committed — they are the reproducible
# provenance record. scripts/ingest-fetched-samples.ps1 is what sifts this
# staging area into samples/ (automated specimen-marker check replacing a
# human PR review, per CONTRIBUTING.md's "Adding a corpus specimen"), then
# scripts/sync-samples.ps1 -Push moves accepted files onto samples-data. This
# directory is the holding pen, not the corpus.
tools/downloaded_passports_and_ids/
tools/downloaded_mrz_documents/
# fetch_commons_mrz_specimens_v2.py resolves its staging dir relative to
# whatever cwd it's invoked from, not always tools/ — ignore both spots.
/downloaded_mrz_documents/
